WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.077
Do we need a completely different index to

00:00:03.097 --> 00:00:05.461
rank the records that passed our Boolean

00:00:05.481 --> 00:00:08.425
search? We already have terms and posting

00:00:08.445 --> 00:00:11.247
lists. What extra evidence would a scoring

00:00:11.267 --> 00:00:12.174
rule need?

00:00:14.084 --> 00:00:16.735
BM twenty-five can use the same inverted

00:00:16.755 --> 00:00:20.073
index. Postings supply within-document

00:00:20.093 --> 00:00:23.101
term frequencies, the dictionary supplies

00:00:23.121 --> 00:00:25.253
document frequencies, and document

00:00:25.273 --> 00:00:27.172
metadata supplies lengths.

00:00:27.335 --> 00:00:30.369
Eligibility and scoring use the same

00:00:30.389 --> 00:00:31.580
underlying map.

00:00:32.492 --> 00:00:35.116
Reuse Chapter Two's four records.

00:00:35.278 --> 00:00:37.797
Our illustrative analyser lowercases the

00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:39.858
words and retains them all.

00:00:40.021 --> 00:00:43.068
Their lengths are four, four, three and

00:00:43.088 --> 00:00:45.606
five terms; the average is four.

00:00:45.768 --> 00:00:48.170
Delulu occurs in two records.

00:00:48.345 --> 00:00:49.961
Job occurs in all four.

00:00:50.518 --> 00:00:54.155
With delulu AND job, only D one and D four

00:00:54.175 --> 00:00:56.655
are eligible. Both use each query term

00:00:56.675 --> 00:00:59.117
once. The data here are a tiny worked

00:00:59.137 --> 00:01:01.539
example, not measurements of a production

00:01:01.559 --> 00:01:02.660
search service.

00:01:03.568 --> 00:01:06.486
Using our positive-IDF version, k one at

00:01:06.506 --> 00:01:09.359
one point two and b at zero point seven

00:01:09.379 --> 00:01:12.053
five, D one scores about zero point eight

00:01:12.073 --> 00:01:15.468
zero. D four scores about zero point seven

00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:17.852
two. Totals are calculated before

00:01:17.872 --> 00:01:18.586
rounding.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.233
Their matching term counts are the same.

00:01:21.395 --> 00:01:24.004
In this example, the length adjustment

00:01:24.024 --> 00:01:25.253
makes the difference.

00:01:25.428 --> 00:01:28.204
These numbers order the two candidates;

00:01:28.392 --> 00:01:30.640
they do not establish which is useful for

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:31.928
a particular reader.

00:01:32.806 --> 00:01:35.094
A top-one request retains D one.

00:01:35.257 --> 00:01:38.136
Top two retains both eligible records,

00:01:38.156 --> 00:01:39.566
with the same scores.

00:01:39.741 --> 00:01:42.582
Top k specifies an output boundary, not a

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:43.715
scoring method.

00:01:44.277 --> 00:01:47.806
A strict AND gate is not compulsory for BM

00:01:47.826 --> 00:01:50.409
twenty-five. Direct retrieval can score

00:01:50.429 --> 00:01:53.244
the union of records matching any query

00:01:53.264 --> 00:01:56.246
term. Our job-only records then compete

00:01:56.266 --> 00:01:59.494
too, giving the order D one, D four, D

00:01:59.514 --> 00:02:03.115
three, D two. Eligibility, scoring and

00:02:03.135 --> 00:02:05.937
output size remain separate controls.

00:02:06.843 --> 00:02:09.140
PubMed's published Best Match architecture

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.284
starts with BM twenty-five, then uses

00:02:12.304 --> 00:02:15.390
LambdaMART to rerank the top five hundred

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.289
candidates. The chapter also describes

00:02:18.309 --> 00:02:21.730
Scopus and Primo CDI documentation of

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:23.972
lexical evidence with additional ranking

00:02:23.992 --> 00:02:24.861
signals.

00:02:25.404 --> 00:02:28.412
Only the PubMed example identifies BM

00:02:28.432 --> 00:02:29.945
twenty-five directly.

00:02:30.121 --> 00:02:33.296
The other accounts do not prove plain BM

00:02:33.316 --> 00:02:34.520
twenty-five ranking.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.776
A relevance label can conceal field

00:02:36.796 --> 00:02:40.023
weighting, phrases, proximity, static

00:02:40.043 --> 00:02:42.136
signals and later reranking.

00:02:42.298 --> 00:02:44.340
These are the chapter's cited accounts,

00:02:44.489 --> 00:02:46.247
not fresh service tests.

00:02:47.145 --> 00:02:50.260
Selective and ordered access still help.

00:02:50.422 --> 00:02:53.160
Compression and blocks reduce storage and

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.332
memory traffic.

00:02:54.508 --> 00:02:56.936
Valid score upper bounds can avoid scoring

00:02:56.956 --> 00:02:59.075
candidates that cannot enter the leading

00:02:59.095 --> 00:02:59.977
results.

00:03:00.526 --> 00:03:02.722
In this separate illustration, a full

00:03:02.742 --> 00:03:04.770
top-k has cutoff five.

00:03:04.932 --> 00:03:07.438
A block with a valid upper bound of three

00:03:07.458 --> 00:03:08.700
cannot compete.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.497
A bound of seven requires inspection; it

00:03:11.517 --> 00:03:13.224
does not guarantee a winner.

00:03:13.399 --> 00:03:15.505
Safe pruning preserves the result under

00:03:15.525 --> 00:03:16.716
the scoring rule.

00:03:16.891 --> 00:03:18.945
No speedup is measured here.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:22.402
Ask what admits candidates, what scores

00:03:22.422 --> 00:03:24.528
them and where the output stops.

00:03:24.677 --> 00:03:28.059
BM twenty-five is a lexical baseline, not

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:30.108
the name of every relevance sort.

00:03:30.296 --> 00:03:33.678
Hardware, index design and query mix also

00:03:33.698 --> 00:03:36.307
affect performance; there is no universal

00:03:36.327 --> 00:03:37.556
speed guarantee.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.629
Next, Chapter Four changes the admission

00:03:40.649 --> 00:03:42.948
question: what happens when a query term

00:03:42.968 --> 00:03:45.242
is optional or matches nothing?

00:03:45.417 --> 00:03:47.420
The same index can support behavior that

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.126
strict AND alone would never predict.
